<section xml:id="sec-gates-switch">
  <title><pf>switch</pf> Gates</title>


<subsection xml:id="sec-switch-gates">
	<title><pf>switch</pf> statement</title>
	
	<p>
		The switch statement is another option for controlling code in MATLAB. It works similarly to the if-statement by directing your code to go down one of many possible paths.

	</p>

	<p>
		The switch statement determines which task to perform based on possible values of some variable. The conditions we use with switch statements are in the form of cases. 
	</p>

	<aside>
		<p>
			Like the if-statement, you can have as many branches as you like. You would just add additional <pf>case</pf> statements between the <pf>switch</pf> and <pf>otherwise</pf> statements.
		</p>
	</aside>

	<assemblage xml:id="switch-statement-def">
		<title><pf>switch</pf>-statement</title><p/>

		<p>
			Suppose you need to perform different tasks based on some possible values of the variable <pf>var</pf>. More specifically, suppose you want your code to perform tasks A, B, C, and D, under the following conditions:
		</p>

		<sidebyside margins="10%">
			<tabular>
				<col	width="100%"></col>
				<row><cell>perform task A if <pf>var</pf> equals <pf>v1</pf>,</cell></row>
				<row><cell></cell></row>
				<row><cell></cell></row>
				<row><cell>perform task B if <pf>var</pf> equals <pf>v2</pf> or <pf>v3</pf>,</cell></row>
				<row><cell></cell></row>
				<row><cell></cell></row>
				<row><cell>perform task C if <pf>var</pf> equals <pf>v4</pf>, or</cell></row>
				<row><cell></cell></row>
				<row><cell></cell></row>
				<row><cell>perform task D for all other cases.</cell></row>
				<row><cell></cell></row>
				<row><cell></cell></row>
			</tabular>
		</sidebyside>

		<p>
			Then the switch commands required to control this flow are given by
		</p>

		<sidebyside width="100%">
			<program language="matlab">
				<code>
					switch var

						case {v1}
							
							Task A commands

						case {v2, v3}
					
							Task B commands

						case {v4}

							Task C commands

						otherwise

							Task D commands

					end
				</code>
			</program>
		</sidebyside>

		<p>
			Notes:
			<ul>
				<li>MATLAB performs ONLY the tasks in the FIRST true case. The rest of the tasks are ignored.</li>
				<li>When there is only one possible value for a case, the curly braces <pf>{ }</pf> are optional.</li>
			</ul>
		</p>

	</assemblage>

	<p>
		The <pf>switch</pf>-<pf>case</pf> structure is similar to the <pf>if</pf> structure, but offers a few advantages.	First, it is easier to read; second, it is better when comparing strings (of possibly different lengths). The basic format is:
	</p>

	<example xml:id="ex_switchexample">
		<title><em>Using if</em></title>

		<statement>

			<p>
				Let's check whether a cadet is in their first two years at VMI.
			</p>

			<sidebyside width="100%">
				<program language="matlab">
					<code>
						Year = 'second class'; 
						switch Year
							case{'fourth class', 'third class'}
								'You are in the first two years.'
							case{'second class', 'first class'}
								'You are in the last two years.'
							otherwise
								'You must be a fifth year.'
						end
					</code>
				</program>
			</sidebyside>

			<p>
				The cases are grouped by curly brackets so that a case will be satisfied if the value of Year is any of the values in a specific case. Once this code is executed, the switch command will look at the value of Year, and the output should be
			</p>

			<sidebyside width="100%">
				<program language="matlab">
					<code>
						You are in your last two years.
					</code>
				</program>
			</sidebyside>

		</statement>

	</example>

</subsection>

</section>
